From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bert Belder <bertbelder@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Expose the thread's current event loop pointer

Every lookup of a thread's event loop starts from one thread-local
pointer, which kj writes in enterScope() and leaveScope() and exposes no
other way. An embedder that runs code it does not trust on a thread that
has a loop cannot leave the host reaching the loop through that pointer:
thread-local storage is ordinary memory the code can write, so the loop
the host arms events on, takes the cross-thread executor from and reads
the event-loop locals of would be one that code chose.

saveCurrentLoopPointer() and restoreCurrentLoopPointer() let such an
embedder hold the pointer in a frame the untrusted code cannot reach for
as long as it runs, and put it back when the code returns. The restore
writes the pointer whatever it holds then, including when it has been
cleared, so it cannot go through enterScope(), whose requirement that
the thread have no loop is what WaitScope relies on.

The event the thread is currently firing gets the same pair, for the
same reason. EventLoop::turn() sets it around Event::fire() and clears
it when fire() returns, so what untrusted code called from inside a
fire() leaves there is read by the rest of that fire(): by ~Event(),
which compares it with the event being destroyed and aborts the process
on a match, and by getAsyncTrace(), which dereferences it.
---
diff --git a/src/kj/async.c++ b/src/kj/async.c++
index 9ca3b2a..ce134ff 100644
--- a/src/kj/async.c++
+++ b/src/kj/async.c++
@@ -1970,6 +1970,22 @@ void EventLoop::enterScope() {
   threadLocalEventLoop = this;
 }
 
+EventLoop* EventLoop::saveCurrentLoopPointer() {
+  return threadLocalEventLoop;
+}
+
+void EventLoop::restoreCurrentLoopPointer(EventLoop* loop) {
+  threadLocalEventLoop = loop;
+}
+
+_::Event* EventLoop::saveCurrentlyFiringEvent() {
+  return threadLocalCurrentlyFiring;
+}
+
+void EventLoop::restoreCurrentlyFiringEvent(_::Event* event) {
+  threadLocalCurrentlyFiring = event;
+}
+
 EventLoop::Id EventLoop::id() const { return Id(loopId); }
 
 EventLoop::Id EventLoop::Id::current() { return currentEventLoop().id(); }
diff --git a/src/kj/async.h b/src/kj/async.h
index 04d619c..622ab10 100644
--- a/src/kj/async.h
+++ b/src/kj/async.h
@@ -1527,6 +1527,30 @@ public:
   Id id() const;
   // Returns the unique identifier of this EventLoop.
 
+  static EventLoop* saveCurrentLoopPointer();
+  static void restoreCurrentLoopPointer(EventLoop* loop);
+  // Read and put back the pointer through which this thread finds the event loop running on it.
+  // Every lookup of the current loop starts from that pointer: the loop currentEventLoop() and
+  // Id::current() find, the one getCurrentThreadExecutor() takes the executor from, and the one
+  // an event is armed on. It is ordinary thread-local storage, so an embedder that runs code it
+  // does not trust on a thread that has a loop can hold the pointer in a frame that code cannot
+  // reach for as long as it runs, and put it back when the code returns, rather than reaching
+  // the loop afterwards through a pointer the code may have changed.
+  //
+  // restoreCurrentLoopPointer() writes the pointer whatever it holds at the time -- a loop the
+  // untrusted code chose, or nothing at all -- which is why it is not enterScope(): the
+  // requirement that the thread have no loop yet, which WaitScope relies on, cannot be made
+  // here. Give it only a pointer saveCurrentLoopPointer() returned on the same thread.
+
+  static _::Event* saveCurrentlyFiringEvent();
+  static void restoreCurrentlyFiringEvent(_::Event* event);
+  // The same for the event this thread is currently firing, which turn() sets around
+  // Event::fire() and clears once fire() returns. Until then ~Event() compares it with every
+  // event destroyed on this thread, from a noexcept function that aborts the process on a match,
+  // and getAsyncTrace() dereferences it and calls through its vtable. An embedder that runs
+  // untrusted code from inside a fire() puts this back as well, so that the remainder of that
+  // fire() does not run on what the code left here.
+
